speechtext:
Motion agreed to.
[Later:}
The house having reverted to motions:

Hon. IAN A. MACKENZIE (Minister of Veterans Affairs): moved:
That -a select committee he 'appointed: 1. To consider all! legislation passed since the commencement of the war with the German ireich relating to the pensions, treatment, and reestablishment of former members of His Majesty's [DOT]armed forces and of other persons who have otherwise engaged in pursuits closely related to war;
2. To prepare and bring in one or more bills to clarify, amend or supplement the above legislation;
With power to send for persons, papers, and records; to print its proceedings, and to report from time to time to the house;
That the provisions of section 1, standing order 65, :be waived in, respect to this committee.
And that the said committee shall consist of the following members: Messrs. Abbott, Adamson, Archibald. Ashby, Baker, Belzile, Benidick-son, Bentley, Blair, Blanchette, Bridges, Brooks,
Bruce, Olaxton, Oleaver, Cockeram, Croll, Cruick-shank, Dion (Lake 'St. John-Roberval), Dorion, Drope, Emmerson, Fulton, Gauthier (Portneuf), Gibson (Hamilton West), Gillie, Green, Halle, Barkness, Harris (Grey-Bruce), Herridge, Isnor, J'Ubras, Kidd, Langlois, Lapointe, Lennard, Marshall, Mackenzie, Macdonald (Halifax), MacNaught, Merritt, Mitchell, Moore, Mutch, Pearkes, Power, Probe, Quelch, Ross (Souris), Sinclair (Vancouver North), Skey, Tremblay, Tucker, Vdau, White (Hastings-Peterborough), Whitman, Winkler, Winters, Wright.
He said: Mr. Speaker, I should like to have the cooperation of the house in following the same procedure as has been followed with regard to the previous motion. This is practically the same wording that we had in the resolution of a year ago, and the personnel of the committee is the same with the exception of one or two changes which have been made by agreement.
It is intended to continue the excellent work which was done a year ago by the committee on veterans affairs. The committee will review if necessary the three measures that were passed by this house and deal with the Pensions Act, the Veterans Land Act, and the act dealing with fire fighters and supervisors that was before the veterans affairs committee a year ago. It will deal with any new suggestions that were made to the committee, such as amendments to the Soldier Settlement Act, and it will deal also with civilian personnel who are protected now by orders in council but who are not protected by statute. These and other matters which will come before the special committee on veterans affairs will I think have the general support not only of the members of the committee which functioned so excellently a year ago, but of the house generally.
